{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Fire and Ice: Yellowstone Lake's Sublacustrine Vent Field",
    "date": "2026-09-16",
    "lesson": "At West Thumb, Yellowstone Lake sits atop its own caldera, and hydrothermal vents erupt from the lake floor beneath 5-10C water year-round. Chemolithotrophic bacteria there fix carbon from dissolved hydrogen sulfide and hydrogen gas seeping through cracks in the lake bed, just like their deep-ocean cousins at black smokers, except this plume rises into cold, sunlit freshwater instead of the crushing dark. Above the vent, diatoms drift in a cold, dim photic zone that never gets very bright even at noon (Yellowstone's high elevation and long winters keep light and heat both marginal). The lesson is about overlap: the vent chemical plume is confined to a narrow band near the floor, while any photosynthesis is confined to a narrow band near the surface, and freshwater chemolithotrophs occupy the vent band exclusively, unable to use light at all. This is the mirror image of a purple-sulfur Winogradsky column: here the two energy sources never overlap, so nothing exploits both, and the vent community is a closed, chemosynthesis-only world with a diatom bloom drifting obliviously overhead.",
    "citation": "Cuhel & Aguilar, sublacustrine hydrothermal vents of Yellowstone Lake; Yellowstone Center for Resources geothermal reports",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Yellowstone Lake West Thumb sublacustrine hydrothermal vent field, ~5-8C ambient lake water"
  },
